WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.319
JavaScript has no compile-time type safety by default,

00:00:03.319 --> 00:00:07.746
and even TypeScript does not remove the need for runtime validation.

00:00:07.746 --> 00:00:10.563
TypeScript improves developer confidence while writing code,

00:00:10.563 --> 00:00:14.185
but its types are erased when the program runs.

00:00:14.185 --> 00:00:17.002
External data from browsers, APIs, files, queues,

00:00:17.002 --> 00:00:21.831
or storage still arrives as runtime values that must be parsed and

00:00:21.831 --> 00:00:24.649
checked before trusted code relies on them.

00:00:24.649 --> 00:00:29.478
Schema validation provides a practical way to define and enforce expected structure.

00:00:29.478 --> 00:00:34.307
A schema can describe required fields, optional fields, types, formats, length limits,

00:00:34.307 --> 00:00:37.929
numeric ranges, arrays, nested objects, enums, and custom rules.

00:00:37.929 --> 00:00:40.746
Instead of writing many ad hoc checks,

00:00:40.746 --> 00:00:46.782
developers can place a clear validation step at the boundary where data enters the application.

00:00:46.782 --> 00:00:52.417
Libraries such as Zod, Joi, Yup, Ajv, and JSON Schema can support runtime validation.

00:00:52.417 --> 00:00:55.234
They differ in style and ecosystem fit.

00:00:55.234 --> 00:00:58.051
Zod is often used with TypeScript-heavy projects.

00:00:58.051 --> 00:01:02.477
Joi and Yup are common in server and form validation patterns.

00:01:02.477 --> 00:01:04.490
Ajv validates JSON Schema efficiently.

00:01:04.490 --> 00:01:08.916
The best choice depends on the application, team familiarity, framework integration,

00:01:08.916 --> 00:01:12.538
and need to share schemas across services or clients.

00:01:12.538 --> 00:01:14.550
Schema validation still requires judgment.

00:01:14.550 --> 00:01:18.977
Developers should understand whether a library coerces values, strips unknown fields,

00:01:18.977 --> 00:01:24.611
allows extra properties, validates nested objects, or produces errors that are safe to expose.

00:01:24.611 --> 00:01:29.843
The schema is the boundary, but the surrounding code must still enforce authorization,

00:01:29.843 --> 00:01:32.660
use safe APIs, and handle failures predictably.
